The RK3588 eMMC controller is in the PD_NVM power domain. When the
genpd framework manages this domain, it can be turned off during the
early suspend phase. If PD_NVM is powered off before the hibernate
image is written to disk, the eMMC controller becomes inaccessible and
the system hangs.

This is the same class of bug as the RK3576 fix in dwcmshc_rk3576_postinit()
(Nicolas Frattaroli, Collabora). Apply the same workaround for RK3588:
call dev_pm_genpd_rpm_always_on() to prevent runtime power-off of PD_NVM.

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-of-dwcmshc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-of-dwcmshc.c
@@ -804,5 +804,16 @@ static void dwcmshc_rk35xx_postinit(str
 	struct sdhci_pltfm_host *pltfm_host = sdhci_priv(host);
 	struct dwcmshc_priv *dwc_priv = sdhci_pltfm_priv(pltfm_host);
+	struct rk35xx_priv *priv = dwc_priv->priv;
+
+	if (priv->devtype == DWCMSHC_RK3588) {
+		int ret;
+
+		ret = dev_pm_genpd_rpm_always_on(mmc_dev(host->mmc), true);
+		if (ret && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
+			dev_warn(mmc_dev(host->mmc),
+				 "failed to set PD rpm always on, SoC may hang later: %pe\n",
+				 ERR_PTR(ret));
+	}
 
